"Mirror Speculative Decoding: Breaking the Serial Barrier in LLM Inference (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13161v1)\nciteturn3051view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13161v1\",\"pattern\":\"4.1 Evaluation\"}); Total lines: 606\nL26:       3. cite16†Cross-accelerator rendezvous. L27:     4. cite17†3.4 Latency Analysis L28:   5. cite18†4 Experiments L29:     1. cite19†4.1 Evaluation protocol L30:     2. cite20†4.2 Tri-objective analysis with an MT-Bench diagnostic L31:       1. cite21†Findings. L32:     3. cite22†4.3 Effectiveness L33:     4. cite23†4.4 Generalizability across model families L34:   6. cite24†5 Conclusion L35:   7. cite25†References L36:   8. cite26†A Related Works L37:     1. cite27†Speculative decoding with draft models. L38:     2. cite28†Single-model approaches. L39:     3. cite29†Dynamic and adaptive decoding. L40:     4. cite30†Positioning. L41:   9. cite31†B Correctness: Acceptance and Distribution L42:     1. cite32†Acceptance operator (rule-level equivalence). L43:     2. cite33†Distributional equivalence (when the verified draft path is identically distributed). L44:     3. cite34†Sufficient condition for equation . L45:   10. cite35†C Latency and Communication Analysis L46:     1. cite36†Comparison to vanilla SD (per step). L47:   11. cite37†D Extended Ablations & Empirical Analysis L48:     1. cite38†D.1 Batching Effects L196: ### 4 Experiments\nL197: \nL198: We evaluate Mirror-SD on a broad suite of generation workloads under realistic serving constraints, using server-scale decoder-only LLMs that are routinely deployed in production inference stacks across mid to large capacities, and we compare against strong speculative-decoding baselines.\nL199: #### 4.1 Evaluation protocol\nL200: Datasets and tasks. We integrate our approach with the open-source SpecBench framework (cite77†Xia et al., 2024b ) to ensure a fair, reproducible comparison against prior methods. SpecBench provides standardized prompts and pre/post-processing, sampling settings and released configs and seeds (cite77†Xia et al., 2024b ). We report results on multi-turn interactive conversation (MT Bench), translation, summarization, mathematical reasoning, machine translation and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).\nL201: Context and generation lengths follow the SpecBench protocol (cite77†Xia et al., 2024b ).\nL202: Models and baselines. We evaluate Mirror-SD on server-scale targets that are deployable in production inference stacks: Qwen3-14B and Qwen3-32B (cite78†Yang et al., 2025 ), Mistral-24B (cite79†Mistral, 2025 ), and OPT-66B (cite80†Zhang et al., 2022 ). For Qwen targets, we train a 0.6B-parameter draft with 2 segments and 8 tracks and deploy it on 8 NPUs as described in cite13†Section 3.3 .\nL514: Figure 6: Comparison of Speculative Streaming (SS) draft dynamics (left) and resulting speedups (right).\nL515: \nL516: (a) Humanities\nL517: \nL518: (b) Math\nL519: \nL520: (c) Roleplay\nL521: \nL522: (d) STEM\nL523: \nL524: (e) Coding\nL525: \nL526: (f) Math reasoning\nL527: \nL528: Figure 7: Fallback frequency vs. Top-$\\kappa$ and early-exit depth across six tasks (Humanities, Math, Roleplay, STEM, Coding, Math Reasoning). Each panel shows fallback frequency as a function of $k$ for exits at 1/4, 1/2, and 3/4 of depth; smaller values indicate fewer fallbacks and greater reuse.\nL561: #### E.4 Empirical confirmation\nL562: \nL563: cite150†Figure 7 reports fallback frequency as a function of $k$ for early exits at 1/4, 1/2, and 3/4 of depth across six tasks. Two consistent trends emerge:\nL564: \nL565:   * •\nL566: \nL567: Top-$\\kappa$ effect. Increasing $k$ monotonically lowers fallback, with diminishing returns once $\\Omega_{\\kappa}$ saturates. This matches the bound $\\mathsf{FF}\\leq 1-\\Omega_{\\kappa}(\\ell_{e})$ and reflects a higher probability that the draft’s precomputed path already contains the target’s correction.\nL568: \nL569:   * •\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMirror Speculative Decoding: Breaking the Serial Barrier in LLM Inference (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13161v1)\nciteturn3051view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13161v1\",\"pattern\":\"B Correctness\"}); Total lines: 606\nL39:     3. cite29†Dynamic and adaptive decoding. L40:     4. cite30†Positioning. L41:   9. cite31†B Correctness: Acceptance and Distribution L42:     1. cite32†Acceptance operator (rule-level equivalence). L43:     2. cite33†Distributional equivalence (when the verified draft path is identically distributed). L44:     3. cite34†Sufficient condition for equation . L45:   10. cite35†C Latency and Communication Analysis L46:     1. cite36†Comparison to vanilla SD (per step). L47:   11. cite37†D Extended Ablations & Empirical Analysis L48:     1. cite38†D.1 Batching Effects L49:     2. cite39†D.2 Draft-side speedups with speculative streaming L50:   12. cite40†E Fallback Dynamics: Influence of Top-$\\kappa$ and Early-Exit Depth L51:     1. cite41†E.1 Setup and definitions L52:     2. cite42†E.2 Monotonicity in $k$ L53:     3. cite43†E.3 Monotonicity in early-exit depth L54:     4. cite44†E.4 Empirical confirmation L55:     5. cite45†E.5 Practical recommendation L56:   13. cite46†F Additional Experimental Details L57:     1. cite47†F.1 Target and Draft Sharding L58:     2. cite48†F.2 Draft Model Configuration L59:   14. cite49†G LLM Usage Statement L404: ### Appendix B Correctness: Acceptance and Distribution\nL405: \nL406: Let $\\gamma$ be the speculative window length, $N$ the number of transformer layers in the target, and let $A_{t}\\!\\in\\!\\{0,\\dots,\\gamma\\}$ denote the accepted-prefix length at step $t$. Recall that the target’s final next-token distribution is $p^{(N)}(\\cdot\\mid h_{\\cdot})$ and that verification commits the longest prefix of the draft that matches the target’s tokens.\nL407: ##### Acceptance operator (rule-level equivalence).\nL408: \nL409: For any realized draft proposal $\\hat{y}_{t+1:t+\\gamma}$ and realized target tokens $y^{\\mathrm{target}}_{t+1:t+\\gamma}$ (obtained by rolling the target with teacher forcing along the agreed prefix and stopping at the first mismatch), both vanilla SD and Mirror-SD compute\nL410: \nL411:  | $$A_{t}\\;=\\;\\max\\Big\\{r\\leq\\gamma:\\ \\hat{y}_{t+j}=y^{\\mathrm{target}}_{t+j}\\ \\ \\forall j\\leq r\\Big\\}.$$  |  | (11)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDo You Get the Hint? Benchmarking LLMs on the Board Game Concept (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13271v1)\nciteturn3051view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13271v1\",\"pattern\":\"Human\"}); Total lines: 295\nL45: Therefore, the process of clue-giving is inherently more imprecise, complicating a fair evaluation of LLMs (i.e., does the model not know the concept, or is it unable to select and combine seemingly unrelated clues in a meaningful way?). Therefore, in our setup, we implement LLMs solely as the guesser, using human-generated clues as input.\nL47:   1. 1.\nL48: \nL49: Compared to humans, how well can LLMs infer concepts from natural language clues, selected by a human from a constrained vocabulary? To what extent can LLMs interpret information expressed through linguistic formulations that differ from how such concepts are typically represented in their training data?\nL50: \nL51:   2. 2.\nL52: \nL53: Are some categories of concepts (e.g., animals, objects, quotes) easier for models?\nL54: \nL55:   3. 3.\nL56: Is the performance on Concept language-dependent? How well do models transfer abstractions to lower-resource languages?\nL57: \nL58: We hypothesize to observe the following:\nL59: \nL60:   1. 1.\nL61: \nL62: We expect that model performance does not match, but correlates with human performance. Additionally, we expect that reasoning models outperform non-reasoning models on this task.\nL63: \nL64:   2. 2.\nL79: It has also been shown that LLMs as players do not necessarily match human performance, but their performance correlates strongly with difficulty as indicated by humans (cite34†Xiao and Yang, 2024 ).\nL83: However, they do not mention a human baseline. Similarly, the game ‘Codenames’ has been introduced as benchmark to test language nuances and theory of mind, on which LLMs again outperform older methods. However, cite38†Stephenson et al. (2025) note that smaller models do not adhere to the constraints of game rules, and models struggle overall with lateral thinking. On the popular puzzle ‘Wordle’, LLMs again underperform but CoT-prompting increases performance (cite34†Xiao and Yang, 2024 ).\nL124: Therefore, in the dynamic prompt, we prompt iteratively, giving the clues from Player 1 per step (one step = the clues given until the original (human) Player 2 interrupted by making a guess). Then, after each step, we add the incorrect guesses from the human players as well as the LLM’s, and update the clues. In this setting, we allow as many guesses as there were made by humans in the original game. We include the prompts in Appendix cite16†B .\nL132: To examine whether performance varied across types of concepts, we categorized rounds by their first (and most important) clue (e.g., wildlife/animal, object/thing/package, building/construction/city, music/song/note), focusing on the top 20 most frequent categories.\nL133: A Bayesian mixed-effects logistic regression model (GLMM)^{7}^{7} 7 cite55†https://bambinos.github.io/bambi/notebooks/getting_started.html#generalized-linear-mixed-models†bambinos.github.io was then fit with ‘found’ (i.e., whether the concept was found) as the outcome and category as a predictor^{8}^{8} 8 Posterior estimates were obtained with NUTS sampling (4 chains; 1,000 warmup and 1,000 post-warmup draws), and category effects were considered meaningful when their 95% highest-density intervals excluded 0..\nL134: The analysis revealed no meaningful differences across categories, indicating that human performance was consistently high regardless of concept type.\nL135: In comparison, all LLMs perform significantly worse than humans across the board (see Table cite56†2 ). Interestingly, in rounds where both humans and the LLM found the correct concept, the LLM overall needs fewer guesses to do so, suggesting greater efficiency once they are on the right track (see Figure cite57†3 ).\nL136: \nL137: cite58†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Comparison of how many guesses were needed to arrive at the correct concept, for concepts that are found both by humans and the LLM.\nL138: However, in rounds where the correct answer is not found easily, LLMs often get stuck pursuing wrong directions, whereas humans are better at revising their earlier interpretations and still arriving at the correct answer^{9}^{9} 9 For models, we observe a strong negative correlation between found rate and number of guesses (Pearson’s $r$ between -0.68 and -0.74), whereas for humans the correlation is weak ($r=-0.14$)..\nL139: This finding echoes Chollet’s cite59†2019 view of exploration as a key component of intelligence: humans can flexibly revise their hypotheses, but LLMs tend to remain fixated. Since Concept requires interpreting strategic linguistic signals within a shared conceptual space, success depends not only on linguistic fluency but also on adaptive exploration—an area where humans still hold a clear advantage.\nL140: To gauge how models compare to each other and to humans on which concepts they (do not) find, we calculate Pearson’s R on the found rates, based on the static prompt. We find that GPT-OSS-120, the reasoning model, has the strongest correlation with human found rates ($r=0.14$). Especially LlaMA4-Maverick has a low correlation with human performance ($r=0.05$).\nL141: Interestingly, comparing model performance to each other, the correlations are very low, suggesting that different models succeed on largely different subsets of concepts.\nL142: Contrary to our expectations, the dynamic prompt yields worse results compared to the static prompt, even though this prompt entails more information, and provides the added possibility to derive useful information from the interaction between Player 1 and Player 2. Corroborating prior research, this suggests that LLMs struggle with simulating Player 1’s strategic intents when giving the clues in in-context learning.\nL143: Further, as mentioned earlier, even with more explicit representations of the updates in clues and incorrect guesses, LLMs are seemingly unable to update their interpretations of the initial clues, and remain stuck in the wrong direction.\nL144: Both for the static and dynamic prompt, we observe instances where the model repeats its previous (incorrect) answer, even though the prompt is updated with this information. Additionally, there are rounds where the guesses are not valid given the constraints of the highest level clue: for instance, if the first clue is ‘building’, the LLM’s guesses are not buildings.\nL145: Again contrary to our initial expectations, the reasoning model we included in our experiments (GPT-OSS-120B) does not outperform non-reasoning models. In some cases, this is caused because the number of reasoning tokens (n=1,000) were insufficient and the model did not arrive at an answer.\nL146: This finding agrees with previous results that longer reasoning processes, in which the model generates more intermediate tokens, does not necessarily help the model to arrive at useful associations or correcting the erroneous directions (cite60†Yuan et al., 2025 ; cite61†Hassid et al., 2025 ; cite62†Vamvourellis and Mehta, 2025 ).\nL147: Table cite63†3 shows the comparison of human and LLM performance per category (grouped by the first, highest-level clue). To compare LLMs with humans across categories, we extended the GLMM model to include solver (human vs. LLM), high-level-clue category, and their interaction as fixed effects. This allowed us to test whether differences in success rates between humans and LLMs varied systematically by concept category.\nL148: For most models, there is no significant difference, except for Qwen2 72B (worse on ‘expression/quote, speak, word’, and ‘theater/film/camera’), LlaMA4-Maverick (worse on ‘expression/quote, speak, word’), and Mistral-Small 24B (worse on ‘expression/quote, speak, word’, and ‘object/thing/package’). To some extent, this agrees with our initial hypothesis that LLMs would struggle more with infrequent and culturally-dependent concepts, such as expressions and quotes.\nL149: Finally, we observe similar trends for Dutch, French, and Spanish, although the differences are even more pronounced. While human performance remains consistently high (94% found rate), all LLMs perform worse, and the gap relative to humans is larger than in English (see Table cite64†4 ). Between the non-English languages, models seem to perform better on Spanish. This could be a reflection of common internet language distributions, where Spanish has a larger share compared to the other two languages.\nL150: However, the models’ documentation does not provide detailed frequency distributions for non-English training data. Most model specifications list French and Spanish as supported languages, but not Dutch, yet LLMs’ performance on Dutch is not significantly worse than on French. This is particularly surprising for Mistral, given its French origins. Overall, the weak performance of LLMs on non-English Concept games highlights their limited capacity to transfer knowledge for this type of abstraction.\nL151:  | Human  | llama3-70B  | Qwen2.5-72B  | Deepseek-V3  | llama4-maverick  | Mistral-24B  | GPT-4.1 mini  | GPT-OSS-120B\nL152: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL153: Aggregated (static)  | 0.9184  | 0.3469  | 0.2982  | 0.3463  | 0.2747  | 0.2955  | 0.3445  | 0.2792\nL154: Aggregated (dynamic)  | 0.9184  | 0.2533  | 0.2336  | 0.3054  | 0.2246  | 0.2075  | 0.2893  | 0.2318\nL155: Only complete (static)  | 0.9339  | 0.3924  | 0.3496  | 0.3863  | 0.3117  | 0.3337  | 0.3728  | 0.3129\nL156: Only complete (dynamic)  | 0.9339  | 0.2713  | 0.2603  | 0.3190  | 0.2432  | 0.2139  | 0.3068  | 0.2493\nL157: Table 2: Found rates per model on the English dataset. Entire dataset: n=1,103, filtered: n=818.\nL158:  | Human  | llama3-70B  | Qwen2.5-72B  | Deepseek-V3  | llama4-maverick  | Mistral-24B  | GPT-4.1 mini  | GPT-OSS-120B\nL159: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL160: Wildlife, animal (n=83)  | 0.9759  | 0.5662  | 0.5903  | 0.5421  | 0.4337  | 0.5542  | 0.5180  | 0.3855\nL161: Theater, film, camera (n=78)  | 0.9358  | 0.3974  | 0.1282  | 0.2948  | 0.2051  | 0.2307  | 0.2564  | 0.1794\nL162: Expression/quote, speak, word (n=69)  | 0.8550  | 0.1594  | 0.0724  | 0.2318  | 0.0579  | 0.1014  | 0.1304  | 0.2028\nL180: Language  | Split  | Human  | llama3-70B  | Qwen2.5-72B  | Deepseek-V3  | llama4-maverick  | Mistral-24B  | GPT-4.1 mini  | GPT-OSS-120B\nL181: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL182: French  | Aggregated (static)  | 0.9403  | 0.1462  | 0.1583  | 0.2123  | 0.2123  | 0.1438  | 0.2133  | 0.2133\nL183: Only complete (static)  | 0.9457  | 0.1712  | 0.1845  | 0.2460  | 0.2448  | 0.1640  | 0.2376  | 0.2279\nL192: We collected game logs in English, Dutch, French, and Spanish from the online boardgame platform Board Game Arena, providing a valuable human baseline performance. Per language, this results in about 1,000 concepts. We released the data for research purposes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVisual Interestingness Decoded: How GPT-4o Mirrors Human Interests (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13316v1)\nciteturn3051view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13316v1\",\"pattern\":\"3.\"}); Total lines: 324\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:   4. cite8†3 Single Image Interestingness Assessment L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Experimental Design L20:     2. cite10†3.2 “Is This Image Interesting?” L21:   5. cite11†4 Relative Image Interestingness Assessment L22:     1. cite12†4.1 Experimental Design L23:     2. cite13†4.2 “Which image is More interesting?” L24:     3. cite14†4.3 Comparisons and Relation to other Approaches L25:   6. cite15†5 Learning a Computational Model L26:   7. cite16†6 Similarities and Differences in Assessment L77: Outline of the Paper. We aim to explore how the implicit knowledge encoded in LMMs relates to the concept of visual interestingness. Sec. cite8†3 and Sec. cite11†4 present consensus and agreement for single and relative image interestingness assessment, respectively. Sec. cite15†5 uses automatically annotated image pairs from GPT-4o to train a computational model for predicting image interestingness within a learning-to-rank framework.\nL78: Finally, Sec. cite16†6 discusses the similarities and differences between humans and GPT-4o, providing insights into what makes an image interesting.\nL79: ## 3 Single Image Interestingness Assessment\nL80: ### 3.1 Experimental Design\nL81: \nL82: Dataset. We chose images from the photo-sharing platform Flickr as these images capture a wide variety of content from diverse communities, including professionals and amateur users [cite57†24 , cite25†1 ]. To select representative and diverse images, we selected 1,000 images from the Flickr-User dataset [cite25†1 ], equally sampled according to their proposed commonly interestingness score.\nL83: Human Annotations. We conducted our user study using Amazon Mechanical Turk. Human workers were instructed on evaluating image interestingness and describing their choice, with no right or wrong answers – it was their decision if an image matched their interest (intentionally kept very open). More specifically, a randomly selected image was shown along with the question “Is this image interesting to you?” with two response options: yes or no.\nL84: Additionally, they were prompted to provide a brief explanation for their choice. Five Human Intelligence Tasks (HITs) were created for each of the 1,000 images. Each HIT contained one image, and workers were compensated $0.01 per completed task. A total of 258 unique workers participated, with each worker labeling images without seeing the same image more than once.\nL85: For further analysis, we split the dataset $\\mathcal{S}=\\mathcal{C}_{H}\\cup\\mathcal{D}_{H}$. An image belongs to $\\mathcal{C}_{H}$ if four or five participants ($(\\cdot)_{H}$, for human) agree on their response, indicating a high level of consensus. Otherwise, it belongs to $\\mathcal{D}_{H}$, reflecting dissent. Additionally, we define the interestingness label $y_{H}$ as 1 if the majority finds the image interesting and 0 otherwise.\nL86: LMM Annotations. The rapid development of foundation models is remarkable, especially considering how quickly new models are released. In our work, we use state-of-the-art LMMs, specifically OpenAI’s GPT-4o [cite22†32 ] (’gpt-4o-2024-11-20’), Meta’s Llama 3.2 [cite39†13 ] (’Llama-3.2-11B-Vision-Instruct’), , and DeepSeeks Vision-Language VL2 [cite42†43 ] (‘DeepSeek-VL2-tiny’). All of which can take text-image inputs and produce text outputs.\nL87: The models are given the same prompt as the human annotators (the exact prompt is provided at the beginning of the subsequent subsections), with each image being evaluated five times. Analog to human annotations, consistently labeled images belong to sets $\\mathcal{C}_{G}$ (for GPT-4o), $\\mathcal{C}_{L}$ (for Llama 3.2), and $\\mathcal{C}_{D}$ (for DeepSeek) others to the corresponding dissent sets $\\mathcal{D}_{\\{G,L,D\\}}$. Annotation labels $y_{\\{G,L,D\\}}\\in\\{0,1\\}$ are defined by majority vote.\nL88: Asking for Explanations. Asking “why” is used twofold, (i) to ensure quality for the judgments (see discussion for Llama 3.2 and DeepSeek annotations in Sec. cite12†4.1 ) and (ii) to gain deeper insights about how humans and LMMs come to the particular conclusion whether the image is interesting (see Sec. cite16†6 ). Examples are shown in Fig. cite58†2 .\nL94: ### 3.2 “Is This Image Interesting?”\nL95: \nL96: Prompt: Is this image interesting? Answer with one word (yes or no) without punctuation and in lowercase. Add a semicolon without space. Explain why in one sentence without going into detail.\nL97:  | $|\\mathcal{C}_{x}|$  | $y_{x}=1$  | $A^{(H,x)}(\\mathcal{S})$  | $A^{(H,x)}(\\mathcal{C}_{H})$\nL98: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL99: H uman  | 91.9 %  | 99.9 %  | -  | -\nL100: G PT-4o  | 93.9 %  | 95.3 %  | 92.9 %  | 93.6 %\nL101: L lama  | 93.1 %  | 99.8 %  | 97.1 %  | 98.3 %\nL102: D eepSeek  | 76.2 %  | 81.4 %  | 75.3 %  | 77.3 %\nL103: Table 2: Consistency and agreement with human annotation for single image interestingness assessment. Unfortunately almost all images are consistently labeled as interesting.\nL144: Unlike in the single-image study above, people’s responses are less consistent in the paired-image experiment. Set $\\mathcal{C}_{H}$ contains 56.3% of the image pairs, indicating consensus in about half of them. GPT-4o exhibits much higher consistency, with 95.5% of all image pairs in $\\mathcal{C}_{G}$. The overall agreement between GPT-4o and human annotations is $A^{(H,G)}(\\mathcal{S})=66.2\\%$.\nL145: In case of human consensus, the agreement increases to $A^{(H,G)}(\\mathcal{C}_{H})=73.8\\%$, while decreases on the dissent set to $A^{(H,G)}(\\mathcal{D}_{H})=56.5\\%$ – as one would expect. Examples are depicted in Fig. cite66†3 .\nL146: Key Insight. GPT-4o’s annotations are aligned with human judgments, especially when there is consensus among people.\nL147: Group  | Model  | Annotations (Sec. cite11†4 )  | Learning to Rank (Sec. cite15†5 )\nL148:  |  | $A^{(H,x)}$  | $A^{(G,x)}$  | $Acc.^{(H)}$  | $r^{(H)}_{S}$  | $Acc.^{(G)}$  | $r^{(G)}_{S}$\nL149: Human  | Human  | -  | 73.8 %  | 77.5 $\\pm$ 2.5 %  | -  | 72.0 $\\pm$ 3.4 %  | 0.59 $\\pm$ 0.06\nL150: LMMs  | GPT-4o  | 73.8 %  | -  | 73.4 $\\pm$ 3.4 %  | 0.59 $\\pm$ 0.06  | 84.8 $\\pm$ 2.5 %  | -\nL151: CuPL  | 60.3 %  | 60.9 %  | 61.5 $\\pm$ 3.5 %  | 0.34 $\\pm$ 0.07  | 63.2 $\\pm$ 3.1 %  | 0.42 $\\pm$ 0.08\nL152: Computational Models  | CI  | 69.6 %  | 67.6 %  | 69.6 $\\pm$ 3.6 %  | 0.54 $\\pm$ 0.06  | 69.1 $\\pm$ 3.3 %  | 0.52 $\\pm$ 0.06\nL153: Memorability  | 35.5 %  | 39.1 %  | 34.7 $\\pm$ 4.0 %  | -0.42 $\\pm$ 0.08  | 38.3 $\\pm$ 3.6 %  | -0.34 $\\pm$ 0.07\nL154: Aesthetic  | 68.3 %  | 75.1 %  | 69.0 $\\pm$ 3.7 %  | 0.50 $\\pm$ 0.07  | 73.6 $\\pm$ 3.7 %  | 0.67 $\\pm$ 0.06\nL155: Social Interestingness  | #Views  | 61.7 %  | 63.9 %  | 63.4 $\\pm$ 3.4 %  | 0.39 $\\pm$ 0.08  | 66.3 $\\pm$ 3.4 %  | 0.48 $\\pm$ 0.08\nL156: #Favorites  | 66.4 %  | 74.0 %  | 66.3 $\\pm$ 3.2 %  | 0.47 $\\pm$ 0.07  | 69.4 $\\pm$ 3.1 %  | 0.57 $\\pm$ 0.07\nL157: #Comments  | 68.0 %  | 74.8 %  | 66.6 $\\pm$ 3.1 %  | 0.46 $\\pm$ 0.07  | 70.2 $\\pm$ 3.2 %  | 0.58 $\\pm$ 0.07\nL158: Table 3: GPT-4o achieves the highest agreement $A^{(\\cdot,x)}$ among all models using human responses as the ground truth. It also outperforms existing models focused on visual interestingness, related concepts, and social metrics. On the right, we show the model’s accuracy $Acc.^{(\\cdot)}$ on the image pairs.\nL159: The global ranking (measured by the Spearman rank correlation $r_{S}^{(\\cdot)}$) of the test dataset remains consistent, indicating that the learning-to-rank model generalizes beyond pairwise relationships for both human and GPT-4o annotations.\nL160: ### 4.3 Comparisons and Relation to other Approaches\nL161: \nL162: Our annotations are compared to other models and concepts related to visual interestingness prediction (c.f. [cite24†9 ]). All approaches provide a measurement, score, or probability per image, which (is claimed) to be related to the image’s interestingness. An image pair is labeled according to which image yields the higher response.\nL163: Aesthetics [cite67†22 ]. The VILA (Vision-Language Aesthetics) model learns image aesthetics by analyzing user comments alongside images. It models subjective aesthetic judgments by aligning visual features with language and categorizing images according to these learned aesthetics.\nL164: Memorability [cite68†21 ]. A predictive model assigns memorability scores to images. Through large-scale experiments with memory recall tasks, quantified memorability is established as a stable metric across viewers. We use the recent re-implementation from  [cite69†30 ].\nL165: Commonly Interesting Images (CI) [cite25†1 ]. Interestingness is subjective. However, some images appeal to a broader audience and are, therefore, of common interest. A predictive model was trained by analyzing how many unique Flickr users “favored” images from a certain category (i.e., visually similar images).\nL175: Learning-To-Rank. A simple learning-to-rank model can be implemented using a Siamese network architecture with shared weights [cite74†3 ]. As we are using images $\\mathbf{I_{0}}$ and $\\mathbf{I_{1}}$ as input, they are first embedded using CLIP^{3}^{3} 3 We also perform experiments with DINOv2 [cite75†33 ] embeddings. Similar, slightly worse (67.1% for Humans and 68.3% for GPT-4o) results and trends were achieved.\nL265: The llama 3 herd of models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.21783, 2024.\n"
